{:edb/db-before-t 158 :edb/db-after-t 159 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533471 50 #inst "2026-10-11T01:13:13.632Z" 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186078574 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397729 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186087885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397302 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186124867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121762 13194139533471 17592186045585 false] [17592186124867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121766 13194139533471 17592186045585 false] [17592186124867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121770 13194139533471 17592186045585 false] [17592186124867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121774 13194139533471 17592186045585 false] [17592186124867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397577 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186124867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397581 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186124867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397585 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186124867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397589 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186124867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186124867 1071 "$$\nx(x-3)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=-1$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=2$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186124867 1071 "$x(x - 3)(x + 1)y^{″} + (x - 3)y^{′} + x(x + 1)(\\ln x)y = 0$\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.The point $x =-1$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\nThe point $x = 0$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\nThe point $x = 2$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\nThe point $x = 3$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." 13194139533471 17592186045585 false] [17592186124867 1074 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-3)(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-3)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x+1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=-1.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x-3=0$ or where $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is undefined. These occur at $x=3$ and $x\\le 0.$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x)=(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}Q(x)=(x-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3},\n$$\n\nis undefined about $x=-1.$ So, $x=-1$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ but the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3},\n$$\n\nis undefined about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=3,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-3)P(x)=(x-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=3,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}Q(x)=(x-3)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3}=(x-3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=3.$ Therefore, $x=3$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186147512 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397464 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186147512 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397517 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186147512 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397531 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186147535 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397606 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186152300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397365 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186152300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397385 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186157462 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397739 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186157462 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397758 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186166969 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397478 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186166970 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397424 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186166970 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397444 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186166970 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397545 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186167288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164906 13194139533471 17592186045585 false] [17592186167288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164912 13194139533471 17592186045585 false] [17592186167288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164919 13194139533471 17592186045585 false] [17592186167288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397405 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186167288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397412 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186167288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397418 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186167288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186167288 1071 "Let $F(x) = x^{2}$ for $x ∈ R$, and let $f(x)$ be the $2π$-periodic extension of $F$ from $x ∈ [- π, π)$ to $x ∈ R$. The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$f(x)∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx))$.\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient $a_{5}$ below, where $⟨, ⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[- π, π)$.\n\n$(⟨\\sin (5x), \\sin (5x)⟩)/(⟨f(x), \\cos (5x)⟩)$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}" 13194139533471 17592186045585 false] [17592186167288 1071 "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{5}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186167288 1074 "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{a}_{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-10\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={a}_{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{5} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{5} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186217252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397623 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186217252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397638 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186217252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397653 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186221455 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397593 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186221461 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397668 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186221461 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397682 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186221463 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397696 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186221463 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397707 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186221463 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397718 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186222031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220824 13194139533471 17592186045585 false] [17592186222031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220827 13194139533471 17592186045585 false] [17592186222031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220830 13194139533471 17592186045585 false] [17592186222031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220833 13194139533471 17592186045585 false] [17592186222031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397505 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186222031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397508 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186222031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397511 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186222031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397514 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186222031 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186222031 1071 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [0,3) \\\\ -3x, & x\\in [-3,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186222031 1071 "$F(x) = {x^{2}, \\mid x ∈ [0, 3); - 3x, \\mid x ∈ [- 3, 0)$\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x ∈ [- 3, 3)$. Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x ∈ R$.\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x ∈ (-3, 3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x ∈ (-3, 3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $lim_(x → - 3^{+})F(x) = lim_(x → 3^{-})F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139533471 17592186045585 false] [17592186222031 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/18/186106373f9848cfefefb23197e7508497ff3d570f326da2ca342d078682128d.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}-3x=-3(-3)=9\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2}=(3)^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{true}$ since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/89/89c3a597ce1114fee9e775f291cf89e8ca04f61eb39cf2b4c896dd4be8022f09.png)" 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186229156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397775 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186229158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397283 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186229158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397346 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186229175 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397494 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186229176 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397564 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186229181 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397487 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186229184 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397314 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186229184 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397330 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186397283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397284 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186397283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397288 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186397283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397294 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186397283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397297 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186397283 1069 "q-338982" 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186397283 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186397283 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+3y=0,\\,y(1)={y}^{′}(3)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 3\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′}(3) & {y}_{2}^{′}(3)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, we have $det(A)=$ {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, this BVP {{field-4}}." 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186397283 1072 #uuid "76fbcb9f-680c-51b9-b052-efdce55007dc" 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186397283 1074 "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$   \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Robin boundary conditions given by\n\n$$\ny(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(3)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, setting ${\\alpha }_{1}=1,$ ${\\beta }_{1}=0,$ ${\\alpha }_{2}=0,$ ${\\beta }_{2}=1,$ and $a=1,$ $b=3,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′}(3) & {y}_{2}^{′}(3)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-4m+3 & =0 \\\\ (m-1)(m-3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have ${m}_{1}=1$ and ${m}_{2}=3,$ and so the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{′}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}^{′}(x)=3{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1} & {e}^{3(1)} \\\\ {e}^{3} & 3{e}^{3(3)}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}e & {e}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{3} & 3{e}^{9}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =e(3{e}^{9})-{e}^{3}({e}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{10}-{e}^{6} \\\\  & ={e}^{6}(3{e}^{4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has the unique solution }y≡0.$" 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186397284 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397285 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186397284 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397286 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186397284 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397287 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186397284 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186397284 1077 "field-4" 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186397284 1079 #edb/ref 17592186397285 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186397284 1080 #uuid "0c637c93-1ee3-58ef-993c