$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise functions $u(t-2)$ and $u(t-5),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2) & ={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<2 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 2\\end{aligned} \\\\ u(t-5) & ={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<5 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nu(t-2)+u(t-5)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<2 \\\\ 1,\\, & 2\\le t<5 \\\\ 2,\\, & t\\ge 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{5}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the second integral diverges for $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}]}_{2}^{5}+2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s})|}_{5}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}-{e}^{-5s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}(0-{e}^{-5s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-5s}}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s},\\,s>0.\n$$" 13194139533471 17592186045585 true] [17592186397495 