7592186045585 true] [17592186397718 1074 "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{9{\\pi }^{2}}=3\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $2$-periodic function $(L=1)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{1}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=3\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))+x(-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+9{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)=\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6\\pi A=\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ 6\\pi B=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}A=-\\frac{1}{27\\pi } \\\\ B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant compon